WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.529
Here is a question you have not asked about the Spotify database.

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:09.059
Why is it three tables?

00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:12.588
Users, songs, listens.

00:00:12.588 --> 00:00:16.118
Why not one big table with everything in it?

00:00:16.118 --> 00:00:19.647
It turns out that one choice, where each fact lives, is most of what schema design is.

00:00:19.647 --> 00:00:23.207
Start with one big table.

00:00:23.207 --> 00:00:26.768
Every time Mickey plays a song you write a row, and his name and email ride along on every single one.

00:00:26.768 --> 00:00:30.328
The same email, copied over and over.

00:00:30.328 --> 00:00:33.889
Now watch what breaks.

00:00:33.889 --> 00:00:37.449
Mickey changes his email, so you fix every Mickey row, miss one, and now he has two.

00:00:37.449 --> 00:00:41.010
You cannot add a new song until someone plays it.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.570
Delete his last listen, and his email vanishes with it.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:48.131
Update, insert, delete.

00:00:48.131 --> 00:00:51.691
Three ways repetition bites you.

00:00:51.691 --> 00:00:55.239
The fix is simple.

00:00:55.239 --> 00:00:58.787
Pull the repeated facts into their own tables.

00:00:58.787 --> 00:01:02.335
A user's email lives once, in Users.

00:01:02.335 --> 00:01:05.883
A song's artist lives once, in Songs.

00:01:05.883 --> 00:01:09.431
Listens keeps only the events, and points at the other two by their keys.

00:01:09.431 --> 00:01:12.979
Now Mickey's email sits in exactly one place.

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:16.527
Change it there, and every query sees the new one.

00:01:16.527 --> 00:01:20.075
Nothing can drift, because nothing is copied.

00:01:20.075 --> 00:01:23.623
This splitting has a name, normalization, and a strict version called Boyce Codd normal form.

00:01:23.623 --> 00:01:27.171
The idea underneath is just that every fact gets one home.

00:01:27.171 --> 00:01:31.286
And here is the part that ties the whole module together.

00:01:31.286 --> 00:01:35.400
Splitting the table is exactly why you join.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:39.515
When you separate facts to store them safely, a query has to put them back together to use them.

00:01:39.515 --> 00:01:43.629
Every join you wrote in Module one was recombining a normalized schema to answer one question.

00:01:43.629 --> 00:01:47.744
Pull apart to store, join to use.

00:01:47.744 --> 00:01:52.187
There is a trade-off.

00:01:52.187 --> 00:01:56.630
Normalized means each fact once, no anomalies, but you pay with a join every time you read.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:01.073
Sometimes that join is too slow, so you do the opposite on purpose.

00:02:01.073 --> 00:02:05.515
You copy facts back together so a read needs no join.

00:02:05.515 --> 00:02:09.958
That is denormalization.

00:02:09.958 --> 00:02:14.401
It reads faster, and now the copies can drift apart again, so you reach for it only when a real, measured cost forces you.

00:02:14.401 --> 00:02:18.975
So default to normalized.

00:02:18.975 --> 00:02:23.550
You will see the other side at scale in Module five, where copying data on purpose is how a system serves millions of reads.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:28.124
And here is the one rule to carry out of all of this.

00:02:28.124 --> 00:02:32.698
If a column repeats the same value down the rows, it probably wants a table of its own.
